Minimal docker container harness setup --- tiny sardines in
a tiny can if you will --- tailored at keeping agents out of
unwanted credential usage while being flexible and
extensible to the point of being dangerous if you want to.
Who am I to say you can't shoot yourself in the foot?  I'm
not your mother.

It works in a LiteLLM setup, where the API key is injected
by an isolated Caddy gateway container. The sandbox image
has a persistent docker volume for things like installing
harnesses, caches etc. Just think of it as a small Linux
installation that you can use as you normally would.

While minimal, some things are included:

    - python3 (w. numpy, pandas, matplotlib)
    - python3 tooling (uv, poetry, ruff)
    - node, fnm
    - git, jq, ripgrep, wget, sqlite3
    - pi, opencode

o-----------------o
|   QUICK START   |
o-----------------o

If you're on Mac, install something like Colima to get
the docker daemon:

    brew install colima
    colima start

Set environment variables on host machine:

    SANDBOX_PROJECT_FOLDER
        The working directory the sandbox uses.
    GATEWAY_URL
        The LiteLLM endpoint URL.
    GATEWAY_KEY
        The LiteLLM API key.

Build and start the containers:

    docker-compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.dev.yaml up -d --build

Then enter the sandbox using docker exec:

    docker exec -it sardine bash

Install whatever tools you want inside the sandbox as you
normally would. This will persist after reboots etc. To make
it a bit less cumbersome and easier to use, you can create
aliases to get you directly into a harness or tool:

    alias sardine="docker exec -it sardine bash"
    alias pi="docker exec -it sardine pi"

                                          ;,//;,    ,;/
                                         o:::::::;;///
                                        >::::::::;;\\\
                                          ''\\\\\'" ';\

Also, if you think the docker notices are annoying once you
leave the sandbox, set the following environment variable on
the host:

    export DOCKER_CLI_HINTS=false

o--------------------o
|  PR REVIEW SCRIPT  |
o--------------------o

./scripts/sardine-pr is an included script, which is run on
the host machine to clone a PR including comments etc to aid
in agent-supported PR reviews. To run it, run this from
host:

    ./scripts/sardine-pr <owner/repo> <pull-number>

That clones the repo into a host cache, fetches
refs/pull/<n>/head, and writes a self-contained review
folder inside $SANDBOX_PROJECT_FOLDER:

    reviews/<owner>__<repo>__pr-<n>/
        README.md                  orientation for the agent
        meta/pr.json               body, comments, reviews, files
        meta/review-comments.json  inline review comments
        meta/pr.diff               unified diff
        repo/                      checkout of the PR head

Requires python3 (standard library only) and the gh CLI,
authenticated on the host:

    gh auth login

And to make it easier to use, symlink it:

    ln -s $(realpath scripts/sardine-pr) ~/.local/bin

o----------------o
|   PI HARNESS   |
o----------------o

pi is installed by default. To enable dynamic model
discovery and LiteLLM MCP registration:

    mkdir -p ~/.pi/agent/extensions
    cp harnesses/pi/litellm.ts ~/.pi/agent/extensions/

o------------------o
| OPENCODE HARNESS |
o------------------o

opencode is installed by default. To enable dynamic model
discovery and LiteLLM MCP registration:

    mkdir -p ~/.config/opencode
    sh harnesses/opencode/litellm.sh

To refresh on every launch, define an alias like this:

    cp harnesses/opencode/litellm.sh ~/.config/opencode/litellm.sh
    alias opencode='docker exec -it sardine sh -c "sh ~/.config/opencode/litellm.sh >/dev/null; exec opencode"'

